## Proof

We compare
$$f(n)=100^{100^{100^n}} \qquad\text{and}\qquad g(n)=2^{2^{2^{2^n}}}.$$

Both are positive, so we may compare iterated base-2 logarithms. The function that remains larger after taking more logarithms is the one that grows faster.

**First logarithm.** Since $\log_2(a^b)=b\log_2 a$,
$$\log_2 f(n)=100^{100^n}\log_2 100, \qquad \log_2 g(n)=2^{2^{2^n}}.$$

**Second logarithm.** Using $\log_2(xy)=\log_2 x+\log_2 y$ and $\log_2(a^b)=b\log_2 a$,
$$\log_2\log_2 f(n)=\log_2(\log_2 100)+\log_2(100^{100^n})=\log_2(\log_2 100)+100^n\log_2 100,$$
$$\log_2\log_2 g(n)=\log_2(2^{2^{2^n}})=2^{2^n}.$$

So we must compare
$$A(n):=\log_2(\log_2 100)+100^n\log_2 100 \qquad\text{vs.}\qquad B(n):=2^{2^n}.$$

**Third logarithm.**
$$\log_2 A(n)=\log_2\!\bigl(100^n\log_2 100+\log_2(\log_2 100)\bigr).$$
For large $n$ the term $100^n\log_2 100$ dominates the additive constant, so
$$\log_2 A(n)=n\log_2 100+\log_2(\log_2 100)+o(1)=\Theta(n).$$
On the other hand,
$$\log_2 B(n)=\log_2(2^{2^n})=2^n.$$

**Comparison.** We now compare
$$\log_2 A(n)=\Theta(n) \qquad\text{vs.}\qquad \log_2 B(n)=2^n.$$

Since $2^n/n\to\infty$, we have $\log_2 B(n)\gg \log_2 A(n)$, hence $B(n)\gg A(n)$, hence $\log_2\log_2 g(n)\gg \log_2\log_2 f(n)$, hence $\log_2 g(n)\gg \log_2 f(n)$, and finally
$$g(n)\gg f(n).$$

**Intuition.** $f$ is a power tower of *height 3* (three $100$'s, with $n$ sitting at the top exponent), while $g$ is a power tower of *height 4* (four $2$'s, with $n$ at the top). Tower height dominates the base: every extra level of exponentiation overwhelms any constant base advantage. Concretely, after stripping three logarithms, $f$ reduces to something linear in $n$ while $g$ still reduces to $2^n$ — an exponential gap that only widens.

Therefore $g(n)=2^{2^{2^{2^n}}}$ grows faster than $f(n)=100^{100^{100^n}}$ as $n\to\infty$.

